From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 08:22:57 -0500
Subject: feat: full screen beside the camera on the Mac, as a setting (S-392)

On a Mac whose screen has a camera housing, with Settings > Stedding >
Pages and menus > "Use the row beside the camera in full screen" on, full
screen the person starts is Stedding's own: a borderless window over the
whole screen, the menu bar and Dock hidden until the pointer reaches them,
and the address row in the row beside the camera, with the field right of
the housing. A page's own full screen stays macOS's. Off by default
(docs/features/toolbar.md T55-T59, settings T51).

The bookmarks bar is part of the address row's surface (toolbar T61,
GitHub issue 98): it paints nothing of its own, the window paints the
ground or the page's colour under it as under the row, in full screen's
overlay too, its text and icons are the row's, and with the row hidden
it alone is the strip, its buttons on its middle, the page starting
square under it. It had kept Material's own surface, a grey band between
a dark page's row and the page, with dark text on the Mac; and with the
row hidden its buttons were centred against the hidden row's address
field, 18 DIP low, where the page cut them in half.

In macOS full screen the row's page-coloured bar, which the overlay paints,
starts at the page card's left edge (toolbar T32, BACKLOG S-471). It ran to
the edge of the view that holds the card, one card gutter left of the page:
6 DIP beside an open sidebar, in the owner's full screen on 0.4.0 beta 19.

Why: macOS's full screen keeps the row beside the camera for the menu
bar, a black band over the page, and no public switch gives it to the
window; the owner chose layout B, as a setting, on 2026-11-03.
Removable when: macOS lets a full-screen window use the row beside the
camera.
